00:00:37.665 --> 00:00:43.585
Depending on your love of horror stories or your&nbsp;
belief in the supernatural, it might be easy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.585 --> 00:00:50.560
convince you that lakes are full of ghosts. That&nbsp;
as you plunge deeper into these lakes’ depths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.852
you will come across translucent bodies&nbsp;
that come alive when nighttime sets in.&nbsp;

00:00:56.326 --> 00:01:00.406
What you might not expect, however,&nbsp;
is that the ghosts look like this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.971 --> 00:01:04.880
an elongated body with its limbs&nbsp;
all packed close to the head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:11.200
wrenching open and closed like scissors that&nbsp;
propel our spectral friend in jarring motions.&nbsp;

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:17.120
This is Leptodora, a genus of water&nbsp;
flea whose transparent bodies have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:22.519
inspired many of the researchers who&nbsp;
study them to call them “ghost fleas.”

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:30.160
Leptodora are cladocerans—the more scientific&nbsp;
name given to water fleas. And cladocerans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.440
are micro-sized members of the crustacean&nbsp;
family, making the ghost flea a distant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:40.817
approximately centimeter-length relative of&nbsp;
the much less spooky lobsters and shrimp.&nbsp;

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:46.560
The Leptodora is more closely related to&nbsp;
Daphnia, a creature who we’ve come across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.760
before on our journey through the microcosmos.&nbsp;
And for Daphnia, the Leptopora family relation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.720
is kind of an unfortunate one because frankly,&nbsp;
Leptodora don’t care about it. They have more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:03.840
important things to focus on, like for example eating Daphnia.
So if you’re trying to hunt down the ghost flea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.960
the key is to look for lakes that are full&nbsp;
of plankton-eating fish, because those lakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:14.960
tend to be full of planktonic cladocerans like&nbsp;
Daphnia. For James, our master of microscopes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.280
there is a specific lake in Warsaw&nbsp;
that seems to meet the criteria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:26.240
and that has reliably turned up leptodora.
But even when he’s able to track them down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.040
and watch them under the microscope, it can be&nbsp;
difficult for him to maintain them in his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.080
home. They seem to be particularly sensitive&nbsp;
to the conditions of the water they live in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:41.323
but unfortunately we don’t know exactly&nbsp;
what it is they are sensitive to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.894 --> 00:02:48.000
So we’re lucky to be able to see the ones that we can.
Now despite its transparent body, there is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.400
feature of the leptodora that clearly stands&nbsp;
out: the single cyclops eye that wobbles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:01.878
at the front end of the flea, attached to a&nbsp;
tube that extends into the leptodora’s body.&nbsp;

00:03:03.995 --> 00:03:09.760
When you zoom in, you can see slices&nbsp;
that look like thin petals projecting out from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:15.120
the center of the eye. There are around 500 of&nbsp;
these slices, and they are each a crystalline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:21.920
cone structure called an ommatidium. Within&nbsp;
each ommatidium are five cells that help the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:29.680
leptodora see. But just as important is what the&nbsp;
ommatidium doesn’t have: the edges are lacking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:35.510
pigments, which helps the leptodora stay more&nbsp;
transparent to avoid detection by predators.&nbsp;

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:43.120
This eye helps the leptodora move in response to&nbsp;
the light around it, and it may even help the flea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:48.880
find areas with food. But interestingly, when&nbsp;
a group of researchers studied the leptodora’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:55.360
hunting habits in 1989, they found that sometimes&nbsp;
the Leptopora would be swimming and swimming in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:01.440
search of food only to swim right past a perfectly&nbsp;
good meal, as if they couldn’t even see it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.600
And other times, they described the&nbsp;
leptodora attacking random bits of debris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:11.760
or empty cladoceran carapaces, again, as if they&nbsp;
couldn’t even see what they were targeting.&nbsp;

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:18.160
And what that suggested to the researchers is&nbsp;
that perhaps the ghost flea doesn’t use its eye to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:25.200
hunt. Instead, it seems to be primarily a tactile&nbsp;
hunter, using its own body like a bear trap that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:32.880
springs into action upon the slightest touch.
The leptodora’s thoracic legs surround its mouth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.400
creating a sort of trap or basket for its food.&nbsp;
And in fact, this structure has names like&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.478
“feeding basket” or “trap basket” to&nbsp;
describe its purpose so very precisely.&nbsp;

00:04:44.518 --> 00:04:51.600
Leptodora spend the day time at the bottom of&nbsp;
their lakes, hiding out in the low oxygen areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.400
that keep them safe from fish that want&nbsp;
to eat them. And during the night time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:02.785
the leptodora will rise to the surface,&nbsp;
deploying its trap basket body in the process.&nbsp;

00:05:03.233 --> 00:05:08.240
As it swims through the waters and prey brushes&nbsp;
up against them, the leptodora’s body responds on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:13.840
instinct, pulling its abdomen forward and&nbsp;
grasping its legs around its new meal like a&nbsp;

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:19.520
very, very dangerous hug. And from there,&nbsp;
the leptodora uses its mandibles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:24.400
to dig the tissue out of its prey and&nbsp;
consume it, disposing of the unwanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:29.638
bits of carapace that surround their meal&nbsp;
the way we might discard a shrimp tail.&nbsp;

00:05:31.553 --> 00:05:37.520
In some instances, researchers have also observed&nbsp;
male leptodora using their longer antennae—which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:44.000
are much shorter in females—to sweep food into&nbsp;
their feeding basket. This is both a practical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.640
use of antennae, and a very interesting&nbsp;
observation because for most of the year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:57.000
male leptodora don’t actually exist. You could say&nbsp;
that they’re the most ghostly ghost flea of all.&nbsp;

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:05.440
For most of the year, leptodora&nbsp;
reproduce parthenogenetically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:10.800
which means that they simply lay their eggs&nbsp;
without relying on any other fertilization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.280
The female leptodora’s brood chamber fills&nbsp;
with yellowish eggs that are produced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:22.000
asexually. And eventually, those eggs will&nbsp;
hatch into larvae inside the leptodora.&nbsp;

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:27.895
And for most of the year, those hatched&nbsp;
leptodora will be female. But in the fall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.895 --> 00:06:33.760
male leptodora begin to appear. Their main&nbsp;
function is to allow for sexual reproduction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:39.180
which gives the ghost flea a chance to mix up&nbsp;
its genes and produce a more diverse population.&nbsp;

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:44.880
And the eggs that result from sexual reproduction&nbsp;
are different from the parthenogenetic eggs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:50.480
Instead of being kept in the leptodora herself,&nbsp;
these transparent eggs—called resting eggs—are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:54.683
released into the water, where they’ll&nbsp;
eventually sink to the bottom of the lake.&nbsp;

00:06:55.457 --> 00:07:00.931
And the eggs remain until one day&nbsp;
they hatch, a new ghost to haunt its lake.

